<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How High Should a TV Be Mounted?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The simple rule is to put the center of the screen at the eye level of the person watching. Home technology trade site CE Pro <a href="https://www.cepro.com/audio-video/how-high-should-a-tv-be-mounted/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">recommends mounting a TV</a> so the center is at eye level when you are seated, and gives about 42 inches (107 cm) from the floor to the center of the screen as a rule of thumb for a typical sofa. That is the starting point the calculator uses for the Sofa option.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the TV cannot sit at eye level, keep the angle small. CE Pro advises no more than 15 degrees of vertical angle to the viewer, and MantelMount&#8217;s guide to <a href="https://www.mantelmount.com/blogs/news/the-science-behind-eye-level-tv-viewing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">eye-level TV viewing</a> cites THX guidance to keep the vertical angle within 15 degrees of the center of the screen, treating it as a limit, not a target. The calculator checks this angle for you and turns red when it is exceeded.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1536" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-01-living-room.jpg" alt="Black wall-mounted TV above a floating light oak media console in a bright living room, seen from behind a cream sofa" class="wp-image-2544" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-01-living-room.jpg 1536w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-01-living-room-300x200.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-01-living-room-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-01-living-room-768x512.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">TV Height Chart for a Sofa</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This chart assumes a 16:9 TV with the center of the screen at 42 in (107 cm), a typical seated eye height on a sofa. Use it as a quick check, then use the calculator above for your own room.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>TV size</th><th>Screen (W x H)</th><th>Bottom edge</th><th>Top edge</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>32&#8243;</td><td>28 x 16 in (71 x 40 cm)</td><td>34 in (87 cm)</td><td>50 in (127 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>43&#8243;</td><td>37 x 21 in (95 x 54 cm)</td><td>31 in (80 cm)</td><td>53 in (133 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>50&#8243;</td><td>44 x 25 in (111 x 62 cm)</td><td>30 in (76 cm)</td><td>54 in (138 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>55&#8243;</td><td>48 x 27 in (122 x 68 cm)</td><td>29 in (72 cm)</td><td>55 in (141 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>65&#8243;</td><td>57 x 32 in (144 x 81 cm)</td><td>26 in (66 cm)</td><td>58 in (147 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>75&#8243;</td><td>65 x 37 in (166 x 93 cm)</td><td>24 in (60 cm)</td><td>60 in (153 cm)</td></tr><tr><td>85&#8243;</td><td>74 x 42 in (188 x 106 cm)</td><td>21 in (54 cm)</td><td>63 in (160 cm)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bigger screens end up with the bottom edge lower, so check that a 75 or 85 inch TV still clears your media console. If it does not, raise it only as much as you need and check the angle in the calculator.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">TV Height for a Bedroom</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-02-bedroom.jpg" alt="Wall-mounted black TV above a light oak dresser, seen from the foot of a bed with sage green pillows and a throw" class="wp-image-2545" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-02-bedroom.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-02-bedroom-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-02-bedroom-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-02-bedroom-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In bed you usually sit higher and lean back, so a bedroom TV often goes a little higher than in a living room. Eye heights in bed vary a lot with the bed and the pillows, so sit where you watch, measure from the floor to your eyes and enter it under More options. For the rest of the room, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bedroom-storage-ideas/">small bedroom storage ideas</a>.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Mounting a TV Above a Fireplace</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-03-fireplace.jpg" alt="Black TV mounted high above a white fireplace with an oak mantel, tilted slightly down, between built-in shelves" class="wp-image-2546" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-03-fireplace.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-03-fireplace-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-03-fireplace-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/tv-mounting-height-03-fireplace-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A mantel usually forces the screen well above eye level, which is why CE Pro and MantelMount both point to pull-down or tilting mounts for this spot. Tick the fireplace box in the calculator, enter the lowest the bottom of the TV can go (the top of the mantel plus the clearance in your fireplace and TV manuals) and it shows the real viewing angle. If it is over 15 degrees, a pull-down mount, sitting further back or another wall will be more comfortable. Styling the mantel underneath? See our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/christmas-mantel-decorating-ideas/">Christmas mantel ideas</a>.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Use the Calculator</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Pick your TV size.</strong> It is the diagonal, measured corner to corner, that the TV is sold by.</li>


<li><strong>Pick where you sit.</strong> Sofa, low sofa, bed or standing fills in a typical eye height.</li>


<li><strong>Read the answer.</strong> The center, bottom and top of the screen are measured from the floor.</li>


<li><strong>Fine-tune if you like.</strong> Under More options, enter your own eye height, viewing distance, the mounting-hole height for the bracket and any fireplace limit.</li>


<li><strong>Test before you drill.</strong> Tape a paper template the size of the screen to the wall and sit down to check it.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Mount It Safely</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A wall mount only works if it holds. Use a mount rated for your TV&#8217;s weight and screen size, fix it into studs or with fixings meant for your wall type, and follow the TV and mount instructions. The U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission&#8217;s <a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/Safety-Education/Safety-Education-Centers/AnchorItgov" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anchor It campaign</a> advises mounting flat-screen TVs to the wall or to furniture to stop them toppling over, and anchoring TVs that are not wall-mounted, especially in homes with young children.</p>



